{
  "id": "Compound::DB00436 - Gene::762",
  "verbalization": "Bendroflumethiazide, a thiazide diuretic, is consistently linked to the target Ca4 through multiple pathways. The evidence suggests that this drug belongs to the thiazide class of diuretics, which are known to inhibit sodium reabsorption in the distal convoluted tubule of the kidney, leading to increased excretion of sodium and water. This mechanism is supported by the binding of Bendroflumethiazide to Ca4, as observed in multiple paths. The lowest common ancestors across these paths indicate a shared classification within the thiazide family, reinforcing the biological relevance of the interaction. The consistent presence of Ca4 in the binding profiles of various thiazide derivatives suggests a general mechanism of action involving calcium regulation, possibly through the inhibition of calcium transport or receptor activity. The evidence is robust and aligns with established pharmacological knowledge, making the relationship between Bendroflumethiazide and Ca4 a well-supported mechanistic link.",
